{% block content %}

<!-- HERO -->
<div class="cbl-hero">
  <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">The Approach</div>
  <h1 class="page-hero-h1">Community-based learning, and where we fit in it.</h1>
  <p class="page-hero-sub">
    Schools Reinventing Counties is often introduced as a Minecraft program or an esports competition. Underneath both of those, it is a community-based learning experience. This page is for the people who need to know exactly what that means before they can sign off on it.
  </p>
</div>

<!-- WHAT IT IS -->
<section class="sec sec-cream">
  <div class="sec-header">
    <h2 class="sec-h2 sec-h2-dark page-h2">What it is</h2>
    <p class="sec-header-intro">California's framework describes community-based learning as contextual, culturally responsive learning that connects classrooms and communities while developing young people as active participants in a democracy.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="cbl-lead">The short version: students learn from the place they live in, and from the people who live there with them.</p>
  <p class="cbl-body">
    That sounds obvious until you look at how rarely school is organised that way. Most curriculum treats the community as an illustration, something a lesson refers to. Community-based learning treats it as the source material. Students study a real local issue, work with people who hold knowledge about it, and produce something that goes back to the community rather than into a grade book.
  </p>
  <p class="cbl-body">
    It is not a subject. It is a method, and it can carry almost any subject on its back. That is why it shows up inside community schools strategies rather than beside them.
  </p>
</section>

<!-- THE EIGHT ELEMENTS -->
<section class="sec sec-cream" style="padding-top: 0;">
  <div class="sec-header">
    <h2 class="sec-h2 sec-h2-dark page-h2">The eight elements</h2>
    <p class="sec-header-intro">Practitioner materials break community-based learning into eight elements. Schools Reinventing Counties was not reverse-engineered from this list, but it activates most of it, and it is worth being precise about where the fit is strong and where it is partial.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="el-list">

    <div class="el-item">
      <div class="el-num">01</div>
      <div>
        <div class="el-name">Place-based learning</div>
        <p class="el-what">Learning anchored in a specific geography rather than a generic case study.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="el-how">
        <div class="el-how-lbl">In the program</div>
        <p>Every county world is built on that county's actual geography, landmarks and community life. Students recognise the streets.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="el-item">
      <div class="el-num">02</div>
      <div>
        <div class="el-name">Student and community agency</div>
        <p class="el-what">Young people making real decisions that carry weight, rather than completing a task on rails.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="el-how">
        <div class="el-how-lbl">In the program</div>
        <p>Teams choose the challenge, choose the response, and defend it. Nobody on our side decides what the right answer is.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="el-item">
      <div class="el-num">03</div>
      <div>
        <div class="el-name">Relationships</div>
        <p class="el-what">Sustained contact between students and the adults who hold knowledge about a place.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="el-how">
        <div class="el-how-lbl">In the program</div>
        <p>Students meet community voices inside the world and, at the finals, in person. This is the element most dependent on what a school builds around the program.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="el-item">
      <div class="el-num">04</div>
      <div>
        <div class="el-name">Community knowledge and assets</div>
        <p class="el-what">Treating a community as a place with existing strengths rather than a list of deficits.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="el-how">
        <div class="el-how-lbl">In the program</div>
        <p>Students investigate what is already working before proposing anything. The design brief asks what to build on, not only what to fix.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="el-item">
      <div class="el-num">05</div>
      <div>
        <div class="el-name">Civic engagement</div>
        <p class="el-what">Participation in the actual civic life of a place, not a simulation of it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="el-how">
        <div class="el-how-lbl">In the program</div>
        <p>Teams present to judges, local civic leaders and county officials. The proposals go to people with standing to act on them.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="el-item">
      <div class="el-num">06</div>
      <div>
        <div class="el-name">Addressing community needs</div>
        <p class="el-what">Work aimed at a need the community itself recognises.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="el-how">
        <div class="el-how-lbl">In the program</div>
        <p>Challenges come from real county conditions: housing, transportation, sustainability, public space. County partners help set them.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="el-item">
      <div class="el-num">07</div>
      <div>
        <div class="el-name">Equity</div>
        <p class="el-what">Access to this kind of learning for students who are usually last in line for it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="el-how">
        <div class="el-how-lbl">In the program</div>
        <p>Teams are four students, not a whole cohort, so a school can start small. Catalina County opens the same experience to counties without a built world.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="el-item">
      <div class="el-num">08</div>
      <div>
        <div class="el-name">Meaningful assessment</div>
        <p class="el-what">Judging the work by whether it holds up to an outside audience, not only by a rubric.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="el-how">
        <div class="el-how-lbl">In the program</div>
        <p>Students defend design decisions to judges who did not teach them. This is also the element schools most often want to extend with their own assessment.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- COMMUNITY SCHOOLS -->
<section class="sec sec-navy">
  <div class="sec-header">
    <h2 class="sec-h2 sec-h2-light page-h2">Where this sits in a community schools strategy</h2>
    <p class="sec-header-intro">A community schools approach asks a school to work as part of its neighbourhood rather than as a building inside one. Most of that work is structural: partnerships, integrated supports, shared leadership. The part that is hardest to point at is instruction.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid-3">
    <div class="pillar">
      <div class="pillar-num">01</div>
      <h3 class="pillar-title">It gives the strategy a lesson</h3>
      <p class="pillar-body">Community schools plans often describe strong partnerships and then struggle to name what changes inside the classroom. This is a concrete answer to that question, with a deadline and an artifact at the end.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pillar">
      <div class="pillar-num">02</div>
      <h3 class="pillar-title">It runs on existing partners</h3>
      <p class="pillar-body">The county office, local government and community organizations a community schools lead already works with are the same people who judge, host and set challenges here.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pillar">
      <div class="pillar-num">03</div>
      <h3 class="pillar-title">It produces something to show</h3>
      <p class="pillar-body">Student voice is easy to claim and hard to evidence. A build, a recorded pitch and a room of officials who heard it is evidence.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

